; lcnc-suite abort handler for the TWP config.
;
; The suite's normal on_abort (subroutines/probe_basic/on_abort.ngc) does the
; safety tidy-up, and upstream's on_abort_no_twp_reset.ngc carries a
; workaround this machine cannot do without. Pointing ON_ABORT_COMMAND at
; either one alone loses the other half, so this does both.
;
; THE WORKAROUND (upstream, LinuxCNC issues #2745 / #2908): after some aborts
; stat.gcodes and parameter[5250] fall out of sync, so the interpreter's idea
; of the active work system stops matching the machine's. On a TWP config that
; is not cosmetic — G53.x puts the plane origin in G59..G59.3 and then
; switches to it, so a desynced index makes the next G53.x unable to find its
; plane. The symptom is "no TWP defined" with the machine left in limbo,
; cleared only by reloading the program. Cycling through at least three
; indexes and restoring the stored one resynchronises them.
;
; NOTE: remapped codes cannot run here — only plain and custom M-codes.
; This deliberately does NOT reset the TWP state (upstream's default): an
; operator who aborts mid-job usually wants the plane still defined. Use
; on_abort_with_twp_reset.ngc instead to clear it.

o<on_abort_twp> sub

; --- suite safety tidy-up (from probe_basic on_abort) ---
M5   (Spindle Stop)
M9   (Coolant Off)
M50 P1
G90  (Absolute Positioning)
G40  (Cancel Cutter Radius Compensation)

; --- WCS-index resync (from upstream on_abort_no_twp_reset) ---
; store the current offset index
#1 = #5220
; we must switch through at least 3 indexes
G59.3
G59.2
G59.1
; restore the index from above
o101 if [#1 GT 6]
 G[59+[#1-6]/10]
o101 else
 G[53+#1]
o101 endif

o<on_abort_twp> endsub
M2
